• Linear Queue False Overflow: When elements are repeatedly enqueued and dequeued, rear reaches MAX-1 while front moves forward, leaving vacant slots at the front. Even with empty slots, new elements cannot be enqueued because rear == MAX-1.
• Circular Queue Solution: Wraps array ends together using modulo arithmetic:
  - Enqueue: rear = (rear + 1) % MAX; arr[rear] = item;
  - Dequeue: item = arr[front]; front = (front + 1) % MAX;
  - Full Condition: (rear + 1) % MAX == front
  - Empty Condition: front == rear (when initialized front = rear = 0).

```c
struct Node {
    int data;
    struct Node *next;
};
```
• Array: Contiguous memory, fixed size at compile-time, fast O(1) random access by index, costly O(n) insertions/deletions.
• Linked List: Disjoint non-contiguous memory allocated dynamically via malloc, variable size, sequential O(n) access, fast O(1) insertion/deletion once pointer is known.

• Insert at Head (O(1)):
```c
newNode->next = head;
head = newNode;
```
• Delete from Head (O(1)):
```c
if (head == NULL) return Underflow;
struct Node *temp = head;
head = head->next;
free(temp);
```

```c
struct Node* reverseList(struct Node *head) {
    struct Node *prev = NULL, *curr = head, *next = NULL;
    while (curr != NULL) {
        next = curr->next; // Save next node
        curr->next = prev; // Reverse pointer
        prev = curr;       // Advance prev
        curr = next;       // Advance curr
    }
    return prev; // New head
}
```
Time: O(n), Space: O(1) auxiliary.

```c
struct DNode {
    int data;
    struct DNode *prev;
    struct DNode *next;
};
```
• Advantages:
  1. Bi-directional traversal (forward and backward).
  2. Deletion of a given node in O(1) without needing to traverse from head to find predecessor.
  3. Easy insertion before a given node.
• Disadvantage: Extra pointer overhead (memory) and more pointer updates per operation.

• Strategy: Greedy algorithm on graphs with non-negative edge weights.
• Relaxation Condition:
  if (dist[u] + weight(u, v) < dist[v]) {
      dist[v] = dist[u] + weight(u, v);
  }
• Complexity: O(V^2) with simple array; O((V + E) log V) with Min-Heap.
• Limitation: FAILS on graphs with negative edge weights (requires Bellman-Ford O(V·E) algorithm).

